WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.712
This lesson covers tries, the tree built for words and prefixes. It powers autocomplete.

00:00:07.062 --> 00:00:09.825
Think of a giant filing cabinet for words.

00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:15.860
The first drawer is chosen by the first letter, the folder inside by the second letter, and so on.

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:21.824
Words that start the same way share the same drawers. Car and cat share the drawers c and a.

00:00:22.274 --> 00:00:25.966
A small flag on a folder says: a complete word ends here.

00:00:27.316 --> 00:00:32.761
Here is a trie holding the words a, at, and to, drawn as a tree of letter codes.

00:00:33.211 --> 00:00:39.365
Searching for at means: from the root, take the a edge, then the t edge, then check the end flag.

00:00:39.815 --> 00:00:44.645
If an edge is missing, the word is not there, and we stop immediately.

00:00:45.095 --> 00:00:49.680
The cost depends only on the length of the word, not on how many words are stored.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.250
Some vocabulary.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:01:00.606
A node usually stores up to twenty six child pointers, one per letter, or a small hash map when the alphabet is large.

00:01:01.056 --> 00:01:06.722
Search asks whether a full word exists. StartsWith asks whether any word begins with a prefix.

00:01:07.172 --> 00:01:14.706
For autocomplete, each node can also cache its top suggestions, so typing a prefix returns results instantly.

00:01:16.056 --> 00:01:18.971
Word Search Two combines a trie with a grid search.

00:01:19.421 --> 00:01:25.342
Instead of searching for each word separately, we walk the grid once and follow trie edges as we go.

00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:31.748
The moment the trie says no word starts like this, we turn back. That pruning is the whole speed up.

00:01:33.098 --> 00:01:34.955
Interview tips for tries.

00:01:35.405 --> 00:01:40.502
Say why a hash set is not enough: it cannot answer prefix questions cheaply.

00:01:40.952 --> 00:01:47.546
Mention memory: twenty six pointers per node adds up, so compressed tries or sorted arrays are used in production.

00:01:47.996 --> 00:01:53.291
And for systems: IP routing tables use a binary trie for longest prefix match.

00:01:54.641 --> 00:02:00.457
Tries trade memory for fast prefix lookups. Practice Implement Trie, then Word Search Two.
